======================================================================================
Multi-scale gradient from ONE multi-bit circuit
======================================================================================
  Heisenberg N=4, block of 4 params, 2 bits each (2^8 = 256 vertices), 3 centres

        R   ratio w0/w1   (exact 2 if linear)  cos(w0,g)  cos(w1,g)  |w1/(R/2)|/|g|
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     0.20        2.0203                          0.99981    0.99981          0.9530
     0.40        2.0857                          0.99700    0.99700          0.8253
     0.60        2.2116                          0.98509    0.98509          0.6505
     1.00        2.8508                          0.88203    0.88203          0.3027
     1.50       15.1368                          0.36572    0.36572          0.0312

  ratio -> 2.0 at small R and drifting at large R means the coarse/fine
  disagreement IS a usable nonlinearity signal, available from the same
  shots that already produce the gradient.
  cos(w1,g) > cos(w0,g) means the fine bit tracks the true gradient
  direction better - the bias half of the bias-variance dial.
